The program is split into a data section, a bss section, and a text section. The data section holds the strings that get printed the , the output label, and a newline character. The bss section reserves two 32-byte buffers in memory, one to hold the raw input from the user and one to build the result string into.
The text section is where the actual code runs. It starts at the _start label, which is the entry point of the program. The first block uses the write syscall to print the prompt to the terminal, then the read syscall to grab whatever the user types into the input buffer
The convert_loop label is where the ASCII to integer conversion happens. It reads one character at a time from the input buffer, checks if it's between '0' and '9', and if not it jumps to done convert and stops If it is a digit, it subtracts '0' from it to get the actual numeric value, then multiplies the running total by 10 and adds the new digit on This builds up the full number one digit at a time.
At done_convert, the number gets doubled by adding it to itself with add %rax, %rax.
The convert_back label handles the integer to ASCII conversion going the other direction. It divides the number by 10 repeatedly, and each remainder is a digit. Those digits get written right-to-left into the result buffer starting from position 31 and working backwards, since division gives you the least significant digit first. Once the loop finishes, %rsi is pointing at the first character of the number string.
After that the program saves the pointer and calculates the length of the number string, then makes three write syscalls in a row — one for "The double is: ", one for the number itself, and one for the newline. Finally it calls the exit syscall with code 0 to cleanly end the program.
